#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084506 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following:\n\n> *Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots$, where*\n> - *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n> - *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.*\n> *Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how induction can be used to prove matrix identities." :tutorial/id #uuid "a5a687c3-df1f-5c1e-ade0-95d0b1a59130" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7936 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084520 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following result using mathematical induction:\n\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> Thus, our statement is true for $n=1$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k\\geq 1\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\n> We write\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{k + 1}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{k} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{k} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 + 2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2(k + 1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction,\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n> is true for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "de68c3b6-b164-5e23-8be7-7ae241373e6c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9171 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
